An $x$-intercept is a point on the graph that lies on the $x$-axis, so $y = 0$.  We can set $y = 0$ to get a unique value for $x$, namely 1.  Therefore, the graph has $\boxed{1}$ $x$-intercept.

[asy]
size(150);
real ticklen=3;
real tickspace=2;

real ticklength=0.1cm;
real axisarrowsize=0.14cm;
